WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:03.000
College is expensive.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.710
Like, really expensive.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.790
Especially here… in the Unites States of
guns, fast food, and student debt.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.780
It wasn’t always this way.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:18.560
In 1980, the average cost of tuition for one
year at a four year private college was $3,500.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.730
Adjusted for inflation, that’s $10,200 2016
dollars.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:28.910
Today, that same year of college goes for
$32,000—3 times as much as only 36 years

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:29.910
ago.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:30.910
So what’s behind this?

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.020
Well that’s both a complicated and controversial
question.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.109
To understand why college has risen in cost
we first have to understand why college has

00:00:38.109 --> 00:00:39.640
always been expensive.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.289
The biggest fraction of your tuition goes
to salaries.

00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:47.350
The national average student to faculty ratio
is 18 to 1 meaning that for every 18 students

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.090
there is 1 faculty member.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:54.199
The average professor at a private college
makes $126,981 per year.

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:59.300
Divided by 18 that comes out to only $7,054
which doesn’t seem that bad but you have

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.480
to consider that there’s more than just
faculty.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:05.800
There’s also coaches, and gardeners and
kitchen staff and dorm staff and maintenance

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.941
people and admissions staff and marketers
and IT staff and security guards and college

00:01:09.941 --> 00:01:10.941
presidents.

00:01:10.941 --> 00:01:15.050
All together, the salaries and benefits of
all these individuals add up to a whopping

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:19.430
$24,000 of our $32,000 tuition.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:23.500
Salaries tend to be high because college functions
similarly to many other industries—following

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.650
the rules of supply and demand.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.860
The most popular college majors vary over
time because interests change to reflect changes

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:30.960
in culture.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.350
The problem is, you can’t just magically
create new professors.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:37.890
By the time new professors go through their
20 years of education and specialize in a

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:42.380
field, that major will no longer be the popular
one so when a major is popular, colleges have

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.690
to compete to get professors and the only
thing that really matters is money—money

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:51.080
brings more professors, more professors brings
more students, and more students brings more

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:52.080
money.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.090
At the same time, professors in historically
popular majors receive tenure.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:00.070
Tenure is the idea that in order to promote
academic freedom, long-time professors should

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.570
have their job guaranteed until retirement.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:06.930
With professors that legally cannot be let
go, there can often be an abundance of professors

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:08.750
in less popular study areas.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:13.260
So, we still have $8,000 of tuition unaccounted
for.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:18.040
Colleges need places to teach and their infrastructure
is rather unnaturally expensive.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.500
Classes usually only run for eight or ten
hours a day, five days a week, for 40 or so

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.660
weeks a year.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:27.890
That means that classrooms are only in use
23% of the time.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.910
The college still has to pay for the space
the other 77% of the time which of course

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.180
adds up.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.860
Additionally, colleges really like to build
nice buildings because these attract students.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:43.040
The average cost to build a building varies
by location, but it averages between $115

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.830
and $215 per square foot.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:50.610
By contrast, U-Mass Amherst’s Commonwealth
Honors College cost $354 per square foot,

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:56.260
U-Mass Boston’s General Academic Building
No. 1 cost $594 per square foot, and Berkely’s

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:00.470
Lower Sproul Building cost $659 per square
foot.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:04.250
Of course there are other factors contributing
to the high price of college, but staffing

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.370
and buildings are the big two.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:08.660
Now for the reason college has tripled in
price.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:12.480
It’s always been expensive to pay highly
educated people to teach in inefficiently

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.760
used buildings so what has changed in the
last thirty years?

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.040
Well, college is more popular than ever.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.420
In the last 20 years, college enrollment has
increased by 50%.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:26.250
Federal and state funding for colleges has
actually increased to an all time high since

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:31.090
1980 but with the simultaneous increase in
students, per-student funding has hit an all

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.270
time low.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:38.940
In 1990, Ohio’s flagship public university—Ohio
State University—paid for 25% of their budget

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:44.790
with government money while in 2012, only
7% of their budget was paid for by the state.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:48.680
With less federal and state money, students
have to pay more out of pocket to make up

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:49.860
the difference.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.340
Additionally, colleges have changed to appeal
to the millennial generation.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:58.319
According to William Strauss and Neil Howe,
the millennial generation—born between 1982

00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:03.770
and 2004—are characterized by seven core
traits: they are sheltered, confident, team-oriented,

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:08.050
conventional, pressured, achieving, and they
feel special.

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:12.099
Sometimes called “trophy kids,” millennials
tend to be more ambitious and feel more unique

00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:15.770
than the preceding generation which means
that they tend to believe more in “the right

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:17.680
fit” idea for college.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.879
Colleges responded to this new attitude by
adding more and more amenities to help them

00:04:20.879 --> 00:04:23.410
stand out to the growing number of students.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:27.360
All these new amenities required more and
more management which contributed to the boom

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.569
in administration at universities.

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:33.759
According to the US Department of Education,
the number of administrator positions increased

00:04:33.759 --> 00:04:40.099
60% between 1993 and 2009 which is 10 times
faster than the number of tenured professor

00:04:40.099 --> 00:04:41.270
positions.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:44.800
So just as I finished my “Why Flying is
so Expensive” video by telling you that

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.620
flying is not that expensive, I’ll finish
this video by telling you that college is

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:49.889
not that expensive.

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:55.400
85 percent of full-time degree seeking students
at four year universities receive some sort

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:59.350
of financial aid with the average grant hovering
around $15,500.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:03.910
That’s a significant amount, but I’ll
admit there’s still a significant amount

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:08.499
left over, until you consider that going to
college is the single greatest investment

00:05:08.499 --> 00:05:11.249
you can make in your life, especially today.

00:05:11.249 --> 00:05:15.229
People who go to college earn on average 98%
more per hour than those who only have a high

00:05:15.229 --> 00:05:20.630
school diploma and that number is up from
only 40% more 35 years ago.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:23.580
So given that, what’s the true cost of college?

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:27.439
When you look at it holistically, over your
whole life, the cost of going to college is

00:05:27.439 --> 00:05:35.639
not $32,000 a year or $128,000 over four years,
but rather negative $500,000.

00:05:35.639 --> 00:05:40.979
By skipping the opportunity to go to college,
you will forfeit on average half a million

00:05:40.979 --> 00:05:42.969
dollars in potential earnings.

00:05:42.969 --> 00:05:44.150
Go to college kids.

